WHEN WE MET CARLOS
Anri looked at Rio.
Rio looked at the remote like it might bite.
They played that one.
Daylight, this time. Ugly daylight. A concrete strip behind a building that had no right to be called safe. The camera was handheld and tired.
Curtis was fourteen and looked older. Dirt in the braids. A split on his mouth. Eyes too bright in a face that had not slept. Justice must have been holding the lens; it kept drifting, then catching up.
A boy stood in front of them. Younger than both. Mexican. Small in the way grief makes a person take up less air before they even know the sentence.
Curtis did not dress it up.
“Carlos.” His Japanese-fluent mouth was not in this year. English, rough, careful. “We found where they were keeping him. We got there.” A swallow that hurt to watch. “Your dad didn’t make it. I’m sorry. I’m so fucken sorry.”
The boy did not scream. That was worse. His face just folded in on itself, like the news had to find a place to sit.
Justice swore under his breath behind the camera. The shot jerked, then turned — deliberate — onto his own face.
Fourteen, maybe fifteen. Same dirt. Same wrecked look. A cut drying at his hairline. He stared into the lens like he was leaving a note for a future that would try to forget.
“I’m Justice.” Voice hoarse. “This is the part nobody claps for. We told a kid the truth because the lie would have been worse.” His mouth twitched, not a smile. “His dad’s gone. He’s got us if he wants us. That’s the meet. That’s the day.”
The camera tipped back.
Curtis had one hand on Carlos’s shoulder. Not trapping him. Just there.
“You don’t have to decide anything right now,” Curtis said. “You eat. You sit. You don’t walk back into that building. That’s the whole job for today.”
Carlos’s voice came in small.
“…Did it hurt. At the end.”
Curtis’s face did the thing Anri had seen on a rooftop and a sidewalk: the truth edited down to what a person could carry.
“He wasn’t alone when we got close,” Curtis said. “That’s what I can give you.”
Justice, off-lens, quiet:
“We remember him. That’s the other thing we can give.”
The clip ran out on Carlos’s shoulder under Curtis’s hand and Justice’s breathing behind the glass.
USB menu.
Rio had both palms over her mouth.
Anri’s eyes were wet and furious at the television, at Mexico, at a year that had no business sitting on a living-room USB.
“…That’s how they met him,” Rio whispered. “Not a party. Not a crew name. A funeral with no coffin.”
Anri nodded once.
“He was fourteen. Telling a younger boy his father was dead. Looking like that.” She stared at the dark screen. “And Justice turned the camera on himself so it would not only be Curtis carrying the face of it.”
Rio dragged her hands down.
“Exotic,” she repeated, sick. “I said exotic.”
