“I know now.”
They did not open the date file again. They did not hunt for how the father ended. The room had already taken a child and given them a name.
Anri looked at the red cap on the couch.
“This is why a meeting felt like a cage,” she said. “This is why he benches us. He has already been the person who walks out of a dark building and has to say I’m sorry to a boy.”
Rio’s voice roughened.
“Carlos is still in that folder. Grown. In love. Still behind. And Curtis is here handing out keys like he didn’t learn family in a parking lot.”
The remote stayed on the far end of the table.
Two girls. Two keys. A 55-inch that had done enough.
On a USB labeled like travel, Mexico stopped being a country
and became a sentence said to a child.
Rio Kamichika put exotic in the trash.
Anri Sonohara watched fourteen-year-old Curtis do the worst kindness in daylight.
Justice Nightstar put his own wrecked face on the record
so the future could not call it a rumor.
They waited for the man those boys had grown into — and they left
the other file shut.
Rio got up like the couch had burned her.
She crossed to the TV, found the stick, and pulled it free in one motion. The 55-inch clicked back to a blank input. No folders. No year. No parking lot.
She sat down hard.
The USB sat in her fist like evidence she did not want to hold and did not want to put back.
“That’s why nothing phases him.” Her voice was thin, angry at herself more than at the dark screen. “Why did I say exotic? I’m glad he’s not here to hear me say something so ignorant.”
Anri looked at the empty HDMI prompt. Then at Rio.
“You said it because the folder was a word on a list,” she said, quiet. “You didn’t say it after Carlos. That matters.”
Rio shook her head.
“I said it with my mouth in his house. With his key in my pocket. After he told us a meeting felt like a cage.” She stared at the little stick. “Fourteen. Blindfold. Then daylight and a kid’s father. And I used a travel-brochure word like it was a postcard.”
Anri’s hands were still in her lap, key ring catching the light.
“He would not have wanted us to watch the first one at all.” A small breath. “He also left the USB in a television a guest can turn on. That is not the same as permission. We stop. We don’t invent a speech to punish yourself with before he even gets home.”
Rio’s laugh came out wrong.
“I’m not punishing me. I’m just— nothing phases him because the phasing already happened. In a room. In a year. In a language I don’t have.” She set the USB on the table, not in the slot, not in her bag. In between. “And I called it exotic.”
Anri nodded once.
“Then when he comes back, you can say you were wrong about a folder. You don’t have to say you saw his brother’s wrecked face unless he asks.” Her voice thinned. “We already took more than a living room is owed.”
Rio dragged her sleeve across her eyes, irritated that there was anything to drag.
