The USB stayed on the table, unplugged.
Neither of them reached for a picture of him that he had not handed over.
On a couch that had already taken too much of a boy’s year,
Rio Kamichika asked about a map he had not drawn for them.
Anri Sonohara kept the underpass separate from Mexico.
One was a sleeve he lifted.
The other was a file she had the sense to kill.
Anri’s voice came late, like she had to walk around the thought first.
“Maybe we got him wrong.”
Rio looked at her.
Anri kept her eyes on the blank TV.
“I’ve never heard him complain about Mexico. Not once. He’ll say cartel like a weather report. He’ll say he doesn’t phase. He’ll change the subject.” A small breath. “He talks a lot about Justice. Like that’s what really wounded him. Someone he can’t move on from.”
Rio sat with it.
The parking lot had been awful. The date file had been worse. Both were rooms. Justice was a person who had been in every room and then was not.
“…Yeah,” Rio said, quieter. “The USB has a country. His mouth has a brother.”
