The red cap sat on the couch like a stand-in.
Two girls waited for a man who had walked out of worse years and still only went quiet for one person.
On a furnished floor that had learned the difference
between a map and a ghost, Anri Sonohara named the real hole in him.
Rio Kamichika put Mexico back in the stick and left Justice
where Curtis had always left him — in his mouth, unfinished, undead.
Rio stood with the USB pinched between two fingers like it might still play something if she held it wrong.
“I’m going to go put this in his room.”
She went down the short hall. Anri heard the door, then a few seconds of quiet.
“Woah…”
Anri followed.
Curtis’s bedroom was larger than the spare. Queen bed. Boxes still taped from a life that had arrived faster than he had unpacked it. A closet with rows of clothes he had not shown the city yet. The tech helmet’s empty hook. A small smart radio on the nightstand.
And the desk.
A tower humming low. An XLR mic on a boom arm like he recorded more than chat-room silence. Three screens in a clean, expensive curve.
Rio stood in the middle of it with her mouth open.
“The hell does he do in here?”
She shook her head once, answering herself.
“Wait. No. We just had a USB with so much info. And we didn’t navigate it properly.” A thinner breath. “Nice set up.”
She set the stick on the desk.
The lightest tap.
The sleeping middle monitor woke anyway.
The wallpaper filled all three panels before either of them could look away.
Six figures.
All masked.
Not party masks. Tech. Angular. Visors and horns and sealed faces — cousins of the oni helmet he had carried out of the apartment an hour ago. A crew photographed like a poster, like a threat, like a joke that had gotten teeth.
The one in the center was unmistakable even without a mouth.
Height. Shoulders. The way he stood like the ground had already lost an argument. Braids escaping the seal at the edges.
Anri’s voice came out small.
“That’s… them.”
Rio’s eyes cut to the figure at his right.
Same height almost. Leaner. Stance cocked like he was about to flick a forehead through the lens. The mask was different — sharper grin built into the plate — but the body language was the boy from the plane. The boy from the garage. The boy who had turned a camera on his own wrecked face in daylight.
“That’s Justice,” Rio said. Not a question. “Has to be.”
The other four held the flanks. One broad, weight forward — Carlos, if a mask could swagger. One tucked close to him. One tall and irritated even in stillness. One with the camera energy still in the angle of her wrist, like Leona had handed the phone off and stepped into frame last.
No dates on the wallpaper. No caption. Hard to tell the year. Only the fact of six people who had decided the world got their faces last.
Anri’s hand found the doorframe.
“He looks at this when he sits down.” Quiet. “Every time. Before school. After wandering. This is the thing on the glass.”
Rio swallowed.
“And we were in the living room playing folders like a channel.” She did not touch the mouse. She did not wake the other two screens. “Put the stick down. That’s it. We’re done being in his room.”
Anri nodded.
They left the USB on the desk, dead, where a brother in a mask could keep watch over it.
The six stayed on the monitors behind them, sealed, undead, a whole life he had walked away from without saying goodbye.
In a bedroom that still had boxes from a future Justice picked,
Rio Kamichika found the crew the city did not have a rumor for.
Anri Sonohara named them without needing a file.
Curtis Nightstar in the middle.
Justice at his shoulder.
Four more still behind in a country that had not found Japan yet.
The screens held the mask he had taken into the night.
They were back in the main room. The bedroom door stayed shut. The red cap was still on the couch like a person who had stepped out of it.
Rio sat down slowly.
“So he’s not just tired because he’s sleepy.” Her voice was flat. Honest. Ugly. “He’s tired of life.”
Anri’s head snapped toward her.
“Don’t say that.” Soft, and not soft at all. “That means he thinks of—”
She stopped. She had stood on a ledge. She knew the end of the sentence. She would not put it in his kitchen.
Rio did not look away.
“He does, though. The writing is all over the walls in here. The song. The list he won’t explain. The way he talks like college and detective work are boxes to tick.” A thinner breath. “I’m not trying to be cruel. I’m saying we heard it. Pretending we didn’t is the ignorant version again.”
Anri’s hands locked around the key.
“…Don’t make it a fact we get to announce.” Her eyes shone. “If you say it like that, it becomes the only story. It isn’t. He caught me. He told Mika I would see her. He handed us a door and told us to stay. Tired people still do that. People who have already decided don’t hand out keys.”
Rio’s mouth pulled.
“He does think about it. I’m not taking that back.” Then, quieter, the hope she was actually trying to put in the room: “But… I think meeting us — and whoever else here in Ikebukuro — has been changing his mind. At least I hope so.”
Anri let the second half in.
“That I can hold,” she said. “Hope. Not the other sentence. Not in this apartment. Not while he’s walking toward a crowd he compared to a cage.”
Rio nodded once.
“Okay. Then that’s the version we keep. He’s tired. He’s grieving. He wrote a song that scared the speakers. And he still told us to be here when he comes back.” She glanced at the shut bedroom. “If Ikebukuro is changing his mind, we don’t become two more people who only hear the ending.”
Anri looked at the door he had left through.
“When he comes back,” she said, almost to herself, “we let it be an apartment. Coffee. A cap on the couch. Not an intervention we stole off a hard drive.”
“Yeah.”
They sat.
Two keys. One unplugged life. A city meeting neither of them had attended.
On a furnished floor that had heard too much of a chorus,
Rio Kamichika named the tired under the insomnia.
Anri Sonohara refused to let the ledge be the whole man.
Ikebukuro got to keep the hope. The oni helmet was still out there.
Rio picked at a seam on the couch.
“I’m sorry, Anri-chan. For the last few days.” She did not dress it up. “I didn’t know why he keeps people close and walks away when things get too deep. You clearly have a better picture than I do.” A small, crooked breath. “I can see now why he only calls you -chan.”
Anri’s ears warmed. She did not grab the title like a trophy.
“You don’t have to give me that,” she said. “He pulled you off a roof before he knew my name. That is also a picture.”
Rio shook her head.
“That’s a rescue. This is—” She gestured at the shut bedroom, the cap, the dead TV. “You got the underpass. The arm he chose. Mika’s name. The sidewalk he wouldn’t let you turn around on. I got motorcycle rides and tea and an IOU I kept cashing like it made me the main story.” Her mouth pulled. “Then I lectured you at school like you were using him. I didn’t have Mexico. I didn’t have a brother on a wallpaper. I just had a boy who smiled at both of us and I got greedy.”
Anri looked at her hands.
“I was greedy too. I heard Rio in a text and my fists—” She stopped. “I told you I wouldn’t make him choose. I meant it. I still mean it. -chan is not a ranking. It is how his mouth is when he is trying not to run.”
Rio huffed a weak laugh.
“It sounds like a ranking when you’re standing in a hallway losing.”
“I know.” Soft. “I have been the girl in the hallway.”
They sat with that.
Rio’s voice came smaller.
“I walked away from him when he was already walking away from himself. You stayed in the sentence. That’s why the honorific sits on you. Not because I don’t matter. Because you let him be deep without making the depth a cage.” She glanced at Anri. “I’m sorry I made it a fight. I’m sorry I told you to stay away from him like he was a prize. After tonight I sound like an idiot.”
Anri shook her head once.
“You sound like someone who got a key and did not know the house had ghosts.” A beat. “You stayed tonight. You unplugged the stick. You put it in his room instead of your bag. That is not an idiot.”
Rio swallowed.
“…Still only you gets -chan.”
Anri’s mouth ticked, tired and almost kind.
“Then let him keep his language. We keep the apartment. When he comes back, you can be sorry at him if you need to. Not at me as a substitute.”
Rio nodded.
“Okay, Anri-chan.”
The honorific sat between them differently this time. Not a theft. A truce.
On a couch that had seen
too many folders,
Rio Kamichika put down
the last few days
and the hallway
and the prize.
Anri Sonohara did not
take the title
and crown herself.
Curtis Nightstar
was still out
in a mask —
and two girls
finally
shared
the wait
without
keeping score.